brief_summary:
TIPPIC was a single-centre, prospective, observational pharmacokinetic study in critically ill children aged 1 month to 15 years who were admitted to the paediatric intensive care unit (PICU) of Ghent University Hospital with an external ventricular drain (EVD) in place and who received intravenous (IV) paracetamol as part of routine care.

Paracetamol is thought to act in the central nervous system and must cross the blood-brain barrier to reach its target. Severe intracranial pathology (for example traumatic brain injury or brain tumours) can disrupt this barrier, and critical illness and growth alter drug disposition. Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) concentrations after IV paracetamol had not been described in this population. The EVD, in place for clinical reasons, allows repeated CSF sampling.

The study aimed to:

1. describe the pharmacokinetics of paracetamol in plasma and CSF after the first dose and at steady state;
2. measure paracetamol-protein adducts, a marker of exposure to the toxic metabolite NAPQI and of the risk of latent hepatotoxicity;
3. identify covariates of paracetamol pharmacokinetics and develop a population pharmacokinetic model to predict optimal dosing regimens in individual children;
4. describe the influence of blood-brain barrier disruption on paracetamol pharmacokinetics.

Blood and CSF were collected by volumetric absorptive microsampling (10 microliters per sample).
